    slug: "bank-pekao-convert-to-excel",
    title: "Bank Pekao Convert to Excel: Statements Made Simple",
    excerpt: "Bank Pekao statements arrive as PDFs, and the transactions still need to reach a spreadsheet. Here is how to convert them to Excel and keep the numbers exact.",
    featureImage: "/blog/bank-pekao-convert-to-excel.webp",
    author: "David Chen, CPA",
    tags: ["bank pekao", "convert bank statement to excel", "bank statement converter", "polish banks"],
    published: true,
    createdAt: "2026-11-18T06:30:00.000Z",
    updatedAt: "2026-11-18T06:30:00.000Z",
    content: "<p>Bank Pekao is one of Poland's largest banks, and its customers living and working abroad often need their statements in a spreadsheet — for a tax return, a loan application, or a set of books kept in English. The statements download as PDFs, which are fine for reading but useless for sorting and totaling. To <strong>bank pekao convert to excel</strong> is the step that turns those PDFs into a working file, and this guide shows how to do it quickly and keep the numbers exact.</p>\n<p>The process is the same whether you hold a personal account, a business account, or both, and whether you need one month or a full year. Download the digital PDFs from online banking, convert the transaction pages, verify the totals, and import the result into your spreadsheet or accounting software. Done once, the routine takes under an hour for a year of statements.</p>\n<h2>Download Your Bank Pekao Statements First</h2>\n<p>Start in Pekao24 online banking or the PeoPay mobile app. Open the statements section and download the months you need as PDFs, one file per month per account. Choose the digital PDF whenever the bank offers a choice: a digital file converts cleanly because the text is embedded in the file, while a scanned image needs OCR and converts far less accurately.</p>\n<p>Download the full year at once if you can, and name each file by year and month as it arrives. Twelve files with clear names are easy to work through; twelve files all called \"statement.pdf\" are not. Keep the originals in a folder of their own, one folder per tax year, because they are the record your spreadsheet has to match.</p>\n<h2>Convert the Statements to Excel</h2>\n<p>Open the converter on the <a href=\"/\">ConvertStatement home page</a>, upload the first month's PDF, and convert only the transaction pages. Pekao statements typically mix a summary, the transaction table, and several pages of terms; converting only the table pages keeps the spreadsheet free of junk rows. If the tool lets you select the table area, draw the box around the transactions — it is the single biggest accuracy improvement available in any converter.</p>\n<p>Check the first month before converting the other eleven. Open the spreadsheet, look at the first ten rows, and confirm that dates landed in the date column, amounts in the amount column, and descriptions stayed in one piece. Then total the credits and the debits and compare them with the totals printed on the statement. Matching sums mean the conversion is trustworthy; a mismatch means a row was dropped or a number was split, and you should find it now rather than at tax time. For the detailed walkthrough, see our guide to <a href=\"/blog/convert-bank-statement-pdf-to-excel\">converting a bank statement PDF to Excel</a>.</p>\n<p>Repeat for the remaining months and combine them into one workbook. One long table with a month column works best if you plan to filter by category or vendor, while one sheet per month is easier to eyeball. Either way, keep the currency in its own column and never mix złoty and euro amounts in the same total — convert foreign-currency rows to your reporting currency first, and note the rate you used.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"/blog/bank-pekao-convert-to-excel-inline-1.webp\" alt=\"bank pekao convert to excel — bank statements with a laptop showing a spreadsheet\" width=\"1200\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" /><figcaption>Convert one month first and verify the totals before converting the rest of the year.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>Clean Up the Spreadsheet for Your Books</h2>\n<p>Converted statements always need a short cleanup. Dates that arrive as text become real dates with Text to Columns. Amounts with spaces as thousand separators and commas as decimal marks — the Polish format — need to be normalized to the format your spreadsheet expects, or the numbers will sort and sum wrong. Strip the currency symbols, replace the separators, and check the column total against the statement before you go further.</p>\n<p>Then add a category column. A statement tells you what you spent; the category tells you why it matters. Rent, utilities, groceries, travel, and business expenses each belong in their own category, and a consistent list is what makes the year-end totals useful. The same categories work in QuickBooks or Xero later, so the habit you build in the spreadsheet transfers directly when you move to software. If you reconcile in QuickBooks, our guide to <a href=\"/blog/bank-reconciliation\">bank reconciliation</a> shows how the same monthly check works there.</p>\n<h2>Use the Spreadsheet at Tax Time and Beyond</h2>\n<p>At tax time, the spreadsheet is the file the return is built from. Income is the sum of deposits that are business income, expenses are the sum of categorized business spending, and the difference is the profit the return reports. Keep the spreadsheet beside the original PDFs, one folder per year, and every number in the return traces back to a row and a line on a statement.</p>\n<p>The same file helps outside tax season too. A lender asking for proof of income accepts a categorized year of transactions faster than a stack of PDFs, and a business owner tracking cash flow can see at a glance which months ran thin. The monthly routine behind it is small: convert, check the totals, categorize while the purchases are still fresh, and file the originals away.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"/blog/bank-pekao-convert-to-excel-inline-2.webp\" alt=\"bank pekao convert to excel — person checking converted transactions at a desk\" width=\"1200\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" /><figcaption>Normalize Polish number formats before totaling, or the sums will come out wrong.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How do I convert a Bank Pekao statement to Excel?</h3>\n<p>Download the digital PDF from Pekao24 or PeoPay, convert only the transaction pages with a PDF to Excel converter, and check the column totals against the statement before using the spreadsheet. Convert one month first to confirm the layout is clean, then convert the remaining months the same way.</p>\n<h3>How do I handle Polish number formats in Excel?</h3>\n<p>Polish statements use a space as the thousand separator and a comma as the decimal mark, so 1 234,56 means one thousand two hundred thirty-four and fifty-six hundredths. Strip the spaces, replace the comma with a period, and format the column as a number before totaling. Check the column sum against the statement total to confirm the conversion.</p>\n<h3>Can I convert a whole year of Bank Pekao statements at once?</h3>\n<p>Most free converters handle one file at a time, so convert month by month and combine the results into a single workbook. The per-month routine with a totals check is the reliable way to do it free, and it catches dropped rows that a bulk conversion would hide.</p>\n<h3>Do I still need to keep the original PDFs?</h3>\n<p>Yes. The spreadsheet is a working copy; the PDF is the record. Keep both for at least three years after the filing date, which is the normal audit window in most countries. Store them together, one folder per tax year, backed up in two places.</p>\n<p>Download the year, convert one month, check the totals, and repeat. That is the whole routine, and it turns a folder of Bank Pekao PDFs into a spreadsheet your books can actually use. Start from the <a href=\"/banks/bank-pekao\">Bank Pekao converter page</a> and convert this year's statements today.</p>",
